WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.859
One question to start: decide, or enforce?

2
00:00:03.139 --> 00:00:07.043
Lesson 1 introduced the split that organizes this whole lesson.

3
00:00:07.323 --> 00:00:11.995
Some extensions add things the model reads and decides what to do with: CLAUDE.

4
00:00:12.275 --> 00:00:14.749
md, rules, skills, output styles.

5
00:00:15.029 --> 00:00:22.475
Others are things the harness does regardless of what the model decides: hooks, permission rules, the sandbox, managed settings.

6
00:00:22.755 --> 00:00:29.027
Subagents and MCP servers sit to one side: they change who does the work and what tools exist.

7
00:00:29.307 --> 00:00:40.976
Claude Code’s features overview gives a trigger for each feature, and most teams add them in roughly this order as needs appear: Claude gets a convention or command wrong twice: add it to CLAUDE.

8
00:00:41.256 --> 00:00:42.067
md.

9
00:00:42.347 --> 00:00:47.488
You keep typing the same prompt to start a task: save it as a user-invocable skill.

10
00:00:47.768 --> 00:00:51.565
You paste the same playbook for the third time: capture it as a skill.

11
00:00:51.845 --> 00:00:59.013
You keep copying data from a system Claude can’t see: connect an MCP server (or give it the system’s CLI).

12
00:00:59.293 --> 00:01:05.117
A side task floods the conversation with output you won’t need again: route it through a subagent.

13
00:01:05.397 --> 00:01:08.981
You want something to happen every time without asking: write a hook.

14
00:01:09.261 --> 00:01:13.379
A second repository needs the same setup: package it as a plugin.

15
00:01:13.659 --> 00:01:17.371
Notice what isn’t on the list: setting everything up on day one.

16
00:01:17.651 --> 00:01:30.600
Each extension costs context, attention, or maintenance, and the best-practices doc warns that if you find yourself at forty hooks and deny rules, most are probably preferences the model could have weighed itself.
